  "text": " To solve this problem, we need to analyze the given properties of the 11-sided polygon \\(A_1A_2 \\ldots A_{11}\\) and find the value of \\(A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11}\\).\n\nFirst, let's denote the lengths of the sides of the polygon as follows:\n\\[A_1A_2 = a_1, \\quad A_1A_3 = a_2, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad A_1A_{11} = a_{10}.\\]\n\nWe know that the area of each triangle \\(A_iA_1A_{i+1}\\) is 1 for each \\(2 \\leq i \\leq 10\\). This implies that the area of the entire polygon can be expressed as the sum of the areas of these triangles minus the overlapping areas, but since the polygon is simple and non-convex, we can use the fact that the area of each triangle is 1 to find the area of the polygon.\n\nThe perimeter of the polygon is given as 20. Therefore, we have:\n\\[a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{10} + A_1A_{11} = 20.\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11}\\). To do this, we will use the cosine rule in each of the triangles \\(A_iA_1A_{i+1}\\).\n\nFor each triangle \\(A_iA_1A_{i+1}\\), the cosine rule gives us:\n\\[a_i^2 = A_1A^2 + A_{i+1}A^2 - 2 \\cdot A_1A \\cdot A_{i+1} \\cdot \\cos(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}).\\]\nGiven that \\(\\cos(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = \\frac{12}{13}\\), we have:\n\\[a_i^2 = A_1A^2 + A_{i+1}A^2 - 2 \\cdot A_1A \\cdot A_{i+1} \\cdot \\frac{12}{13}.\\]\n\nSince the area of each triangle is 1, we can use the formula for the area of a triangle:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot A_1A \\cdot A_{i+1} \\cdot \\sin(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}).\\]\nThis implies that \\(\\sin(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = \\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)^2} = \\frac{5}{13}\\).\n\nThus, the area of each triangle is:\n\\[1 = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot A_1A \\cdot A_{i+1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{13},\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[A_1A \\cdot A_{i+1} = \\frac{26}{5}.\\]\n\nNow, substituting this into the cosine rule, we get:\n\\[a_i^2 = \\left(\\frac{26}{5}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{26}{5}\\right)^2 - 2 \\cdot \\frac{26}{5} \\cdot \\frac{26}{5} \\cdot \\frac{12}{13} = \\left(\\frac{26}{5}\\right)^2 \\left(1 - \\frac{12}{13}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{26}{5}\\right)^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{13} = \\frac{52}{25}.\\]\nThus, \\(a_i = \\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{5}\\).\n\nSince the perimeter is 20, we have:\n\\[a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_{10} + A_1A_{11} = 20.\\]\nSubstituting \\(a_i = \\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{5}\\), we get:\n\\[10 \\cdot \\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{5} + A_1A_{11} = 20,\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[4\\sqrt{13} + A_1A_{11} = 20,\\]\nso:\n\\[A_1A_{11} = 20 - 4\\sqrt{13}.\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{13} + 20 - 4\\sqrt{13}}{1} = \\frac{20 - 2\\sqrt{13}}{1} = \\frac{20 - 2\\sqrt{13}}{1}\\).\n\nThus, \\(m = 20\\), \\(n = 1\\), \\(p = 2\\), and \\(q = 1\\). Therefore, \\(m + n + p + q = 20 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 24\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{24}.\\]",
